  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Style Inconsistencies**\n\nI\'ve observed a style discrepancy: the `.btn-primary` class lacks `display: block;` and `text-decoration: none;` in both CSS and HTML files. This is due to a recent change where a `<button>` was replaced with an `<a>` tag, indicating the styles now need adjustment to maintain consistent visual behavior and design.\n\n\n**Considering User Journey Flow**\n\nI\'m now contemplating the user journey when they click \"Entrar com o Gov.\" My concern is a dual navigation issue: wanting both `gov.br` and the dashboard. Attaching `onClick` might silently navigate to the dashboard, which feels odd. Without it, the user might be stuck. The phrasing from the user\'s request, similar to the \"Radar de Mercado Mundial\" request, underscores a direct link intention to `gov.br`.\n\n\n**Refining UX Navigation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Entrar com o Gov\" flow. I\'m leaning toward a solution:  using a standard `<a>` tag with `target=\"_blank\"` for the gov.br link, adding the required styling as inline styles, and integrating `onClick` to navigate to the dashboard. The `Ajuda` and `Contato` links will remain unchanged. This approach handles the external link and internal navigation gracefully without user obstruction.\n\n\n**Evaluating Tab Navigation**\n\nI\'m now wrestling with the tab navigation after a user clicks \"Entrar com o Gov.\"  My key thought is this: would they expect a background tab for `gov.br`, or might that surprise them?  It feels like the standard OAuth approach, opening `gov.br` in a new tab while keeping the portal accessible, is the ideal user experience. A test is in order to confirm this.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_7430439195418129334"
