  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the User\'s Request**\n\nI\'m delving into the specifics of the user\'s currency conversion request. I\'m focusing on the desired behavior: an immediate display of the converted amount to Peruvian Sol upon clicking \"convert\". I\'m also inspecting the implementation within `/src/App.tsx` (lines 11-15) to understand the current code.\n\n\n**Examining Relevant Files**\n\nI am now closely examining `/src/App.tsx` (lines 1-110, 245-275) and `/public/brapex_portal.html` (lines 426-441, 1100-1140). I have already checked `/src/App.tsx` (lines 11-15), which defined the initial state as BRL to PEN, with a value of 100 BRL to 68 PEN. Now, I am exploring the change handlers and calculations to determine how the conversion is performed, as well as the event handling, and rendering code. I aim to identify any logical flaws or opportunities for improvement in this area.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Conversion Logic**\n\nI\'m now deeply engaged with the `converterMoeda` function and the related JSX elements. I\'m focusing on lines 252-267 of the JSX to understand the presentation and formatting of the conversion results. I see the direct manipulation of the display value within `setPenVal` and, I am assessing its impact on the user\'s perception of the process. I am verifying the currency symbol placement and formatting.\n\n\n**Assessing User Input Interactions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the behavior of `handleBrlChange` and its interactions with the input field. I\'m taking into account how the automatic updating of `penVal` during typing impacts the user experience. I am also paying attention to comma use within the input value, and how that impacts the conversion. I\'m considering scenarios where the user enters values with a comma or other formatting, and how the system interprets those entries.\n\n\n**Addressing Input Formats**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the input handling. I\'ve realized the `<input type=\"number\">` might be problematic with comma separators common in Brazilian Portuguese; this might be the core issue. I\'m testing whether this rejects commas or misinterprets them. I\'m also considering the implications of switching to `<input type=\"text\" inputMode=\"decimal\">` or handling both comma and decimal formats properly. The current live update logic might be confusing for users expecting a \"Convert\" button action.\n\n\n\n**Investigating UX Weaknesses**\n\nI\'m now focusing on critical usability issues. The \"Converter\" button lacks visual feedback after conversion, as the `onChange` event provides live updates. Additionally, I\'m analyzing the currency label situation and the direction of conversion. The lack of currency labels on the left input and unclear indication of which box represents which currency, along with the misleading subtitle, make it confusing.\n\n\n"
