    thinking: "**Verifying Current Exchange Rate**\n\nI\'m currently verifying the precise, real-time BRL to PEN exchange rate (`1 R$ = ? S/.`). I also need to understand the user\'s request for the currency converter: \"In the currency converter part. I want that when the person clicks on convert. The correct converted amount for the Peruvian sol already appears.\"\n\n\n**Analyzing Code Functionality**\n\nI\'m now diving into the code, specifically `/src/App.tsx`, to see how the BRL to PEN conversion currently works. I\'m focusing on lines 259-264 to understand the UI elements and event handlers. I see the input fields for BRL and PEN, the \"convert\" button, and how the values are being passed around. I\'m also looking at the current exchange rate and how it\'s handled, to ensure I\'m using the latest rate.\n\n\n**Clarifying UI Behavior**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the UI behaves with the \"Converter\" button, especially concerning the currency conversion and the `handleBrlChange` function. I see that the UI should display the converted amount only after the button click, as the user wants. I need to make sure the input handles both integer and decimal values. The UI output should clearly show the converted amount, like `R$ 100,00 = S/ 68,00 (Sol Peruano)`.\n\n\n**Investigating Conversion Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the conversion logic to ensure accurate output, especially considering the user\'s focus on the correct converted amount in Peruvian Sol. I\'m focusing on the current rate of `0.68` and the `converterMoeda()` function from the HTML file, comparing it to the code in `src/App.tsx`. I see potential differences in how inputs are handled and formatted, especially with decimal values, and will delve deeper into how the exchange rate is being applied. I\'ll search the web for the real BRL to PEN rate.\n\n\n"
